__registerContent("newspaper/polity/2026-07-24/rti-corruption", {"notes":{"news-2026-07-24-rti-corruption":{"newsType":true,"date":"24 July 2026","paper":"The Hindu","subject":"Polity / GS-2 (Governance, RTI, Anti-Corruption)","summary":"A dialogue between Shailesh Gandhi (former Central Information Commissioner) and Anjali Bhardwaj (RTI activist) on whether corruption is India's biggest threat. Key arguments: RTI Act has been systematically weakened by Supreme Court judgments and bureaucracy, DPDP Act 2023 is being used to undermine RTI, Information Commissioners are being appointed non-transparently, and institutional accountability bodies (CBI, ED, Lokpal) have been compromised.","keyPoints":[{"heading":"RTI Act — Strengths and Erosion","points":["RTI Act 2005: considered one of the best transparency acts in the world; ~6 million RTI requests filed annually in India (highest globally)","The RTI Act has been 'murdered by Supreme Court judgments and bureaucracy' — Shailesh Gandhi","Original RTI Act: personal information of public activity or privacy intrusion could be denied, but with caveat that information SHALL NOT be denied to Parliament or State legislatures","Girish Ramchandra Deshpande SC judgment: said all personal information could be exempt — misinterpreting the original caveat","Puttaswamy judgment (privacy as fundamental right): while correct in itself, created a theory allowing government to refuse sharing all personal data","RTI vital in exposing: Vyapam scam, Adarsh Housing Society scam, electoral bond scheme"]},{"heading":"DPDP Act 2023 vs RTI","points":["DPDP Act 2023 (Digital Personal Data Protection Act): originally RTI had 87-word definition of personal information; DPDP has modified it so that all information relating to corruption can be denied","A lot of countries do not have vigilance departments; India has them in almost every government department — but they don't work","The amendment by DPDP Act should be taken back — Anjali Bhardwaj","Section 19 of Prevention of Corruption Act: originally required sanction for prosecution of public servants; in 2018, Section 17A was introduced requiring government permission to investigate corruption charges"]},{"heading":"Information Commissioners — Appointment Crisis","points":["Since May 2014 (BJP came to power): NOT a single Information Commissioner has been appointed to the central information commission till people approached the Supreme Court","Appointments are made in a completely non-transparent manner","Need: People who can deliver transparency should be appointed","RTI should take it on itself to track 10-15 celebrity corruption cases and see how they progressed"]},{"heading":"Weak Institutions Enable Corruption","points":["CBI, ED, and Lokpal have been 'completely compromised' — big-ticket corruption cases just not being tackled","2008 Indian Police study: CBI anti-corruption branch performance (1980-1984) — 280 people accused, 144 convicted; average time for investigation = 13.4 months; first trial = 88 months; only 4 people in prison for more than 20 days","Today, corruption is 'all-profit, negligible-risk activity': so long as this continues, there will be people who take advantage","India is at 79th position in the Rule of Law Index — no hope of being among top nations without addressing corruption","Need: all corruption cases disposed of in less than a year","Lokayukta: been there for years but not delivering; Lokpal: burning ₹50-60 crore per year, delivering nothing"]},{"heading":"Cross-links","points":["GS-2 Governance: RTI Act 2005, DPDP Act 2023, Prevention of Corruption Act, CBI, ED, Lokpal","GS-2 Judiciary: SC judgments on RTI (Deshpande, Puttaswamy), judicial interpretation of transparency","GS-4 Ethics: Corruption as ethical failure, whistleblower protection, integrity in public life","Essay: 'Transparency and accountability — cornerstones of good governance in India'","Mains: 'The Right to Information Act has transformed India's governance landscape but faces structural threats. Discuss.'"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"RTI Act 2005 (Right to Information Act)","explanation":"Central Government legislation enacted on 12 October 2005. Empowers citizens to request information from public authorities. Key features: 30-day response time (48 hours for life/liberty); two-tier appeal system; Central/State Information Commissions as quasi-judicial appellate bodies; penalties for non-compliance up to ₹25,000. India has ~6 million RTI filings per year — highest globally. Has been instrumental in exposing: Vyapam scam, 2G spectrum scam, Adarsh Housing scam, CWG irregularities, Electoral Bond scheme."},{"term":"DPDP Act 2023 (Digital Personal Data Protection Act)","explanation":"India's first comprehensive data protection legislation enacted August 2023. Governs the processing of digital personal data. Key provisions: consent-based data processing, Data Fiduciary obligations, Data Protection Board of India as adjudicating body, right to erasure and correction. Controversy: Section 44(3) amends the RTI Act to allow denial of information on any personal information basis — critics argue this weakens RTI by allowing governments to refuse corruption-related information by classifying it as 'personal data'."},{"term":"Section 17A, Prevention of Corruption Act (added 2018)","explanation":"Introduced in 2018 via Prevention of Corruption (Amendment) Act. Requires prior sanction (government permission) from a competent authority before a police officer can investigate any offence alleged to have been committed by a public servant under the Act. Critics argue this creates an additional layer of executive protection for corrupt officials, since the government (which employs the accused) must give permission to investigate the accused."},{"term":"Lokpal","explanation":"India's national anti-corruption ombudsman established by Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act 2013. Became functional only in 2019 when Justice Pinaki Chandra Ghose was appointed as the first Lokpal. Has jurisdiction over the Prime Minister (with limitations), Union Ministers, MPs, and Group A officers. Critics point out it has very limited actual enforcement — no convictions in its years of existence. Budget: approximately ₹50-60 crore/year."},{"term":"Rule of Law Index","explanation":"An annual index published by the World Justice Project measuring how the rule of law is experienced by the general public across 142+ countries. It covers eight factors: constraints on government powers, absence of corruption, open government, fundamental rights, order and security, regulatory enforcement, civil justice, and criminal justice. India's 2023 rank: 79th out of 142 countries — indicating significant room for improvement, particularly on corruption and judicial access."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["Corruption in India is not merely a law enforcement problem — it is an institutional architecture problem. The RTI Act gave citizens a tool, but judicial overreach (Deshpande judgment) and legislative dilution (DPDP Act 2023 amendment) have systematically weakened it. When the very instruments of accountability are captured, corruption becomes self-reinforcing.","The 'all-profit, negligible-risk' characterisation of corruption captures a fundamental failure of deterrence: when CBI investigations take 13+ months, trials take 88+ months, and barely 4 people out of 280 accused spend more than 20 days in prison, rational actors in public life see corruption as a low-risk activity.","The non-transparent appointment of Information Commissioners (requiring SC intervention since 2014) is a case study in how governments can hollow out accountability mechanisms without formally abolishing them. A transparent, merit-based appointment process — similar to how the CJI is appointed — is essential.","The DPDP Act 2023's Section 44(3) amendment to the RTI Act is particularly alarming: by expanding what can be classified as 'personal information' to include virtually all government data, it potentially allows corruption-related disclosures to be denied on privacy grounds — inverting the RTI's purpose.","Systemic reform requires: (1) reversing the DPDP amendment to RTI, (2) transparent appointment of Information Commissioners, (3) eliminating Section 17A's prior sanction requirement, (4) time-bound disposal of corruption cases (< 1 year), (5) independent functioning of CBI, ED, and Lokpal free from executive interference."],"prelims":["RTI Act 2005: enacted 12 October 2005; ~6 million filings/year in India (highest globally)","DPDP Act 2023: India's first data protection law; Section 44(3) amends RTI — allows denial of personal information","Section 17A PC Act: requires prior government sanction before investigating public servants (added 2018)","Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act: passed 2013; first Lokpal (Justice P.C. Ghose) appointed 2019","India's Rule of Law Index rank: 79th (World Justice Project)","Girish Ramchandra Deshpande SC judgment: expanded scope of 'personal information' exemption under RTI","Lokayukta: state-level anti-corruption ombudsman; Lokpal: national-level"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-07-24-rti-corruption":{"title":"RTI & Corruption (24 Jul 2026)","questions":[{"q":"Approximately how many RTI requests are filed annually in India, as mentioned in the article?","options":["1 million","3 million","6 million","10 million"],"correct":2,"explain":"About 6 million RTI requests are filed in India every year — the highest number anywhere in the world."},{"q":"Section 17A of the Prevention of Corruption Act (added in 2018) requires:","options":["Mandatory jail term for all corruption convictions","Prior government sanction/permission before police can investigate a public servant for corruption","Mandatory confiscation of assets of all convicted corrupt officials","Whistleblower protection for those reporting corruption"],"correct":1,"explain":"Section 17A (added in 2018) requires prior sanction from a competent government authority before any police officer can investigate corruption charges against a public servant — critics argue this shields officials."},{"q":"The DPDP Act 2023's amendment to the RTI Act is controversial because:","options":["It makes all government documents secret","Section 44(3) allows information relating to corruption to be denied by classifying it as 'personal information'","It abolished the Central Information Commission entirely","It increased RTI filing fees by 500%"],"correct":1,"explain":"DPDP Act 2023's Section 44(3) amends the RTI Act to allow denial of information on personal information grounds — critics argue this can be used to deny corruption-related disclosures, inverting the RTI's purpose."},{"q":"India's position in the World Justice Project's Rule of Law Index is approximately:","options":["29th","49th","79th","109th"],"correct":2,"explain":"India is at approximately 79th position in the Rule of Law Index — indicating significant challenges in areas like absence of corruption and criminal justice, according to the article."},{"q":"Assertion (A): Corruption in India has become a 'low-risk, high-reward' activity.\nReason (R): Investigation times average 13+ months, first trials take 88+ months, and barely 4 people out of 280 accused in a studied period spent more than 20 days in prison.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R directly explains A — when corruption investigations take over a year, trials take 7+ years, and actual imprisonment is extremely rare, rational actors face minimal deterrence."},{"q":"The Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act was passed in which year, and who was India's first Lokpal?","options":["2011; Justice A.P. Shah","2013; Justice Pinaki Chandra Ghose (appointed 2019)","2016; Justice J.S. Khehar","2018; Justice Dipak Misra"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act was passed in 2013 but became functional only in 2019 when Justice Pinaki Chandra Ghose was appointed as India's first Lokpal."}]}}});
